## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a cloud-agnostic Terraform module with clean separation of concerns, provider aliasing, and robust gating to prevent drift or accidental exposure.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliases and multi-cloud orchestration
- Module abstraction over provider-specific resources
- Per-environment backends and key management
- CI plan gating with JSON plan validation
- Basic test coverage via Terratest for existence and config

## Code Example

```terraform
# Root usage
variable "cloud" { type = string }
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool }
variable "public_read" { type = bool }
variable "environment" { type = string }

provider "aws" { alias = "aws" region = "us-east-1" }
provider "google" { alias = "google" project = var.environment }
provider "azurerm" { alias = "azurerm" features = {} }

module "object_store" {
  source = "./modules/object_store"

  cloud       = var.cloud
  bucket_name = var.bucket_name
  versioning  = var.versioning
  public_read = var.public_read
  environment = var.environment
}
```

```terraform
# modules/object_store/main.tf (skeleton)
variable "cloud" { type = string }
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool }
variable "public_read" { type = bool }
variable "environment" { type = string }

provider "aws" { alias = "aws" }
provider "google" { alias = "google" }
provider "azurerm" { alias = "azurerm" }

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  count = var.cloud == "aws" ? 1 : 0
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = var.public_read ? "public-read" : "private"

  versioning {
    enabled = var.versioning
  }
}

# google and azurerm blocks would mirror the above with appropriate attributes
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to support policy-driven controls (e.g., CFRs) across clouds?
- How would you test drift between environments and prevent accidental exposure in prod?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design environment-scoped Terraform with proper naming hygiene, state isolation, encryption, and CI validation gates - critical skills for production infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- Wrapper module pattern for environment injection (dev/stage/prod)
- S3 bucket with server-side encryption (AES256) enabled by default
- CloudWatch log group for application logs
- Backend state per environment: state/${environment}/logs.tfstate
- CI gate enforcing naming convention (environment in bucket name) and encryption enforcement

## Complete Code Example

**variables.tf (wrapper)**
```hcl
variable "environment" {
  type        = string
  description = "Environment: dev, stage, or prod"
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "stage", "prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "Environment must be dev, stage, or prod."
  }
}
variable "project" {
  type        = string
  description = "Project name prefix"
}
variable "region" {
  type        = string
  default     = "us-east-1"
}
```

**main.tf (child module - s3_logs)**
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "logs" {
  bucket = "${var.project}-${var.environment}-logs"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "logs" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id

  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm = "AES256"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_log_group" "app_logs" {
  name              = "/${var.project}/${var.environment}/logs"
  retention_in_days = var.environment == "prod" ? 90 : 7

  tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
  }
}

variable "project" { type = string }
variable "environment" { type = string }
```

**main.tf (wrapper)**
```hcl
module "logs_infrastructure" {
  source = "./modules/s3-logs"
  project     = var.project
  environment = var.environment
}
```

**backend.tf (partial configuration)**
```hcl
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    key            = "logs.tfstate"
    encrypt        = true
    bucket         = "my-terraform-state-bucket"
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-locks"
  }
}
```

**CI Gate - validate-terraform.yml**
```yaml
name: Terraform Validation
on: [pull_request]
jobs:
  validate:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3
        with:
          terraform_version: 1.6

      - name: Init terraform
        run: terraform init -backend-config="key=state/${{ matrix.environment }}/logs.tfstate"
        env:
          AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}
          AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}
        matrix:
          environment: [dev, stage, prod]

      - name: Validate bucket naming
        run: |
          BUCKET_NAME=$(terraform output -raw bucket_name)
          if [[ "$BUCKET_NAME" != *"${{ matrix.environment }}"* ]]; then
            echo "Error: Bucket name must contain environment: $BUCKET_NAME"
            exit 1
          fi

      - name: Validate encryption enabled
        run: |
          ENCRYPTION=$(terraform output -raw encryption_enabled)
          if [ "$ENCRYPTION" != "true" ]; then
            echo "Error: S3 bucket encryption must be enabled"
            exit 1
          fi
```

**outputs.tf**
```hcl
output "bucket_name" {
  value       = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id
  description = "Name of the S3 bucket for logs"
}

output "log_group_name" {
  value       = aws_cloudwatch_log_group.app_logs.name
  description = "Name of the CloudWatch log group"
}

output "encryption_enabled" {
  value       = true
  description = "Whether SSE encryption is enabled"
}
```

## Evaluation Criteria
- [ ] Wrapper module correctly injects environment context
- [ ] S3 bucket naming includes environment suffix
- [ ] Server-side encryption is enabled (AES256)
- [ ] CloudWatch log group created with appropriate retention
- [ ] Backend configured for per-environment state isolation
- [ ] CI gate validates bucket naming convention
- [ ] CI gate validates encryption is enabled

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect cross-repo blue/green deployments with per-env isolation and safe promotion gates, using remote state data sources across repos.

## Key Concepts
- Blue/Green deployment across environments
- Terraform data sources and remote state cross-repo access
- Per-env backends and promotion gating in CI
- Route53 weighted routing for traffic shifting

## Code Example
```javascript
data "terraform_remote_state" "network" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = { bucket = "infra-network-terraform" key = "prod/terraform.tfstate" region = "us-east-1" }
}
resource "aws_lb_target_group" "blue" { }
resource "aws_lb_target_group" "green" { }
resource "aws_route53_record" "prod" {
  name = "app-prod.example.com"
  type = "A"
  set_identifier = "prod-blue"
  weight = 100
  alias { name = aws_lb_prod.dns_name, zone_id = aws_lb_prod.zone_id, evaluate_target_health = true }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle drift if network outputs change?
- How do you automate health checks before promoting traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design cross-cloud disaster recovery with strict tenant isolation, governance enforcement, and automated validation—a realistic, high-stakes enterprise scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-cloud provider aliasing and per-tenant state backends
- DR readiness flags and retention policies as code
- Shared module registry usage for multi-tenant DR fabric
- Automated testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example
```terraform
# Minimal skeleton showing per-tenant aliasing
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "primary"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "dr"
  project = var.dr_project
}

# Per-tenant state backend
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tenant-${var.tenant_id}-state"
    key    = "primary/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

# DR readiness flag
variable "dr_enabled" {
  type    = bool
  default = false
}

# DR submodule mirrors critical resources to GCP when enabled
module "dr_replication" {
  source  = "../modules/dr-replication"
  count   = var.dr_enabled ? 1 : 0
  providers = {
    aws.primary = aws.primary
    google.dr   = google.dr
  }
}
```

## Testing Strategy
```go
func TestPrimaryCreatesResources(t *testing.T) {
    terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
        TerraformDir: "../fixtures/primary",
    }
    defer terraform.Destroy(t, terraformOptions)
    terraform.InitAndApply(t, terraformOptions)

    assertResourceCount(t, terraform.OutputRequired(t, terraformOptions, "resource_ids"), 1)
}

func TestDRNoOpWhenDisabled(t *testing.T) {
    terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
        TerraformDir: "../fixtures/dr",
        Vars:          map[string]interface{}{"dr_enabled": false},
    }
    plan := terraform.Plan(t, terraformOptions)
    assertEmptyPlan(t, plan)
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to simplify tagging, maintain per-env isolation, and introduce a safe migration path.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform provider default_tags
- Per-env backend prefixes
- Gate checks on plan output for tag presence
- Migration risk and rollback considerations

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  default_tags = {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      CostCenter  = var.cost_center
    }
  }
}
```

```hcl
# migrated resource sample (no wrapper)
resource "aws_vpc" "main" { cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16" }
```

```hcl
# backend example
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "corp-terraform-state"
    key    = "envs/${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle prod tag overrides if needed?
- What tooling would you add to enforce tag presence across environments?
